      <p class="eyebrow">Why LDAR matters</p>
      <h2>Every leak costs you three ways.</h2>
      <p>A leak is a compliance finding, lost gas, and reported methane. Near your crews, it is also a hazard.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="stat-cards">
      <li><p class="stat">&lt;2%</p><p>of well sites produced most well-site methane in 11 of 15 aerial surveys. Find the big leaks first.</p><p class="stat-source">— Sherwin et al., Nature, 2024</p></li>
      <li><p class="stat">30%+</p><p>of fossil methane cuts pay for themselves at 2025 gas prices, and more in oil and gas.</p><p class="stat-source">— IEA Global Methane Tracker, 2026</p></li>
      <li><p class="stat">28x</p><p>Methane's GWP under the GHG Reporting Program. Every open day counts 28 times over.</p><p class="stat-source">— 40 CFR 98, Table A-1</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="marker-note">An LDAR program is only as good as the proof that it ran.</p>
  </div>
</section>

